{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe completion verifier receives only the fenced ticket_context (description + comments, gate_ops.py assemble step) — zero file contents. Every file is discovered agentically: measured across five live verifier runs, 84-158 requests x ~4-6s each, dominated by read_file/search_files round-trips, and all four captured runaway loops were search_files repeat signatures — the model hunting for text it expects to exist. Wall-clock cost is round-trips, not tokens: runs already cache-read 8.7M of 8.8M input tokens, but that history was assembled one tool round-trip at a time. Overlap analysis of the four live gate traces against the closing subtree's file_impact union shows 67-82% of distinct file reads are covered by declared file_impact — the verifier spends its opening requests re-fetching files the tickets already name.\n\n## Approach\n\nPRE-FETCH, do not restrict (operator-ratified direction): append a bounded, clearly-labelled prefetched-content section to the byte-stable leading part of the fenced verifier context — the ticket's declared file_impact contents (for an epic, the subtree union) plus the referencing commits' diffs — while keeping the full tool surface untouched so the agent still explores beyond the prefetch. Assistance, not a ceiling.\n\nRanking rule for the budget clamp (the union can exceed the budget; the e369 subtree union is 112 paths; the prefetch budget is 30,000 tokens, the upper end of the community-validated 8-30k pre-load range): (1) the closing ticket's OWN file_impact first, (2) subtree paths ranked by cross-descendant frequency (a path impacted by more descendants ranks higher), (3) skeleton-compress files over 500 lines (signatures + docstrings) BEFORE dropping any file, drop lowest-ranked last. A PRE-LOAD MANIFEST records per-file full|skeleton so downstream consumers (the duplicate-call damping sibling's in-context nudge) never claim an elided body is in context. The post-clamp coverage is re-measured against the four live traces as an acceptance criterion — the 67-82% figure was measured on the unclamped union and does not carry over.\n\nCache mechanism: the prefetch section rides the message-tail caching arm (capabilities.py:684-696, gated on execution_mode=\"agentic\") with a CachePoint after the fenced block — NOT RunRequest.cache_prefix, which operates on the system prompt only (runner.py:136-144).\n\nThe prefetch section carries an anti-anchoring instruction (prefetched content is a starting point; the verifier confirms against the working tree). Misses in the trace analysis were dominated by test files, so the prefetch set includes a matching-test-glob heuristic (tests/**/test_<module>*.py for each prefetched module).\n\nPhase 0 — measure first: extend the usage recorder to log the raw distinct fetch list per gate run — (tool, path/query) — not just hashed top-repeats. Today's records cannot validate overlap directly: signatures hash full args (line ranges, query strings), and brute-forcing 2,573 tracked paths resolved only 2 of 6 heavy read_file repeats. Record first; tune the prefetch set from measurement.\n\nPhase 1 — prefetch file_impact + referencing-commit diffs, bounded (per-file char cap and the 30k-token aggregate cap mirroring completion_recovery's _MAX_* pattern; oversize files skeleton-compressed with an explicit marker naming the tool call to fetch the rest). The same prefetch section feeds recovery successor runs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py — Phase 0: record raw distinct fetch list per gate run.\n- src/rebar/llm/workflow/gate_ops.py — Phase 1: append bounded, ranked prefetch section + manifest to verifier context.\n- src/rebar/llm/operations.py — assemble_context seam for prefetched file contents + commit diffs.\n- src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_recovery.py — the same prefetch for successor runs.\n- tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_prefetch.py — new: ranking, clamp, manifest, skeleton marker, toolset pin.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Phase 0: every agentic gate run records its distinct fetch list (tool name + primary path/query arg) in the usage log by default; checked by a unit test asserting the recorded shape for a run with known tool calls.\n- [ ] Overlap measurement exists: an analysis entry point reports the fraction of recorded fetches covered by a ticket's file_impact; checked by running it on recorded data.\n- [ ] Phase 1: the completion verifier's context includes a labelled prefetch section with ranked file_impact contents and referencing-commit diffs, bounded per-file and by the 30k-token aggregate cap; unit test pins the assembly (ranking order, bounds, skeleton marker, label, anti-anchoring instruction).\n- [ ] The pre-load manifest records per-file full|skeleton and is exposed to the tool-wrapping seam; unit test pins its shape.\n- [ ] The tool surface is unchanged: the verifier retains read_file/search_files/list_directory; unit test pins the toolset with prefetch enabled.\n- [ ] Recovery successor runs receive the same prefetch section; unit test.\n- [ ] Post-clamp coverage re-measured: the clamped prefetch set's coverage of the four recorded live traces' distinct reads is computed and recorded on this ticket (the unclamped 67-82% does not carry over).\n- [ ] Live Bedrock A/B before ship (session experiment, not in unit suite): a verifier-scale run on a real closed ticket's material with prefetch reduces total request count by at least 20% versus the post-rework baseline for the same ticket, and the verdict class equals the baseline's; both request counts and verdicts recorded on this ticket.\n- [ ] make lint and make typecheck clean; full CI marker suite matches the pre-existing baseline.\n\n## Testing\n\nHeld-out unit oracle for context assembly + toolset pins (python -m pytest tests/unit/workflow/ -q): ranking order over a synthetic subtree, aggregate clamp with skeleton-before-drop, manifest shape, truncation marker text, anti-anchoring instruction presence. Phase-0 recorder shape test alongside existing usage_log tests. Live A/B on Bedrock recorded as ticket comments with usage-log figures."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786047338632854001,"uuid":"28b684ac-6939-4fb9-9eac-d25a3fd9b48e"}